::ENG3_U06_PT_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker begins a presentation with a clear claim and relevant evidence.
What skill is being shown?{
~Decoration#Incorrect. The choice is about oral communication, not decoration.
=Organized oral communication#Correct. A clear claim and relevant evidence support an organized presentation.
~Publication export#Incorrect. Exporting is a publication step, not the oral presentation skill here.
~Random multimedia use#Incorrect. No multimedia choice is being tested.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide image is labeled and connected to the speaker's point.
Why is this effective?{
~It fills space on the slide.#Incorrect. Filling space is not the purpose.
~It replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Media supports evidence but does not replace it.
=It improves audience understanding.#Correct. Labeled, connected media helps the audience follow the message.
~It avoids audience needs.#Incorrect. The choice responds to audience needs.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes writing for a class LAN page.
What should be checked before publishing?{
=Audience fit, clarity, multimedia support, source information, and conventions#Correct. These checks support publication readiness.
~Only the number of images#Incorrect. Images are not the only publication concern.
~Only the background color#Incorrect. Color does not determine writing quality.
~Only whether the file is long#Incorrect. Length is not a quality measure.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q04::MLA.ENG3.RS.01
Context: A research summary uses a source but does not explain its connection to the claim.
What is missing?{
~Decoration#Incorrect. The issue is research integration, not decoration.
=Synthesis of the source with the claim#Correct. Research must be connected to the interpretation or purpose.
~A larger font#Incorrect. Font size does not explain source relevance.
~A new unrelated topic#Incorrect. A new topic would weaken focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q05::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a paragraph is unclear.
What should the student do?{
~Ignore the feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback supports revision.
~Change only the color.#Incorrect. Color does not fix unclear writing.
=Revise the wording and organization for clarity.#Correct. Revision should improve clarity and structure.
~Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q06::MLA.ENG3.V.01
Context: A student uses academic vocabulary in a presentation.
Which use is best?{
=Use precise terms and explain them when needed.#Correct. Academic vocabulary should support audience understanding.
~Use difficult words only to sound advanced.#Incorrect. This can confuse the audience.
~Avoid all vocabulary related to the topic.#Incorrect. Precise vocabulary helps communication.
~Use terms without connection to the claim.#Incorrect. Vocabulary should support the message.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q07::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A slide caption has spelling errors.
What should happen before submission?{
~Leave the errors because slides are informal.#Incorrect. Presentation text should follow conventions.
=Correct the errors to support clarity and credibility.#Correct. Standard English matters in digital communication.
~Delete the caption even if it explains the image.#Incorrect. A corrected caption may be useful.
~Ignore the caption because only images matter.#Incorrect. Captions help explain multimedia.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker lists evidence but never states a claim.
What is the main problem?{
=The audience may not understand the presentation's focus.#Correct. A claim gives the evidence purpose.
~The presentation has too much audience awareness.#Incorrect. The issue is missing focus.
~The speaker used too many captions.#Incorrect. Captions are not the issue.
~The file is already published.#Incorrect. The context is oral presentation planning.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A chart on a slide is too small to read.
What should the student do?{
~Keep it small so all details fit.#Incorrect. If the audience cannot read it, it does not help.
=Simplify and enlarge the key information connected to the claim.#Correct. This improves audience experience and understanding.
~Add unrelated graphics.#Incorrect. Unrelated graphics distract.
~Remove all labels.#Incorrect. Labels help the audience.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A published summary has no title.
Why is that a problem?{
~Titles are never useful.#Incorrect. Titles guide readers.
~A title replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
=A title helps the audience understand the purpose before reading.#Correct. Audience-aware publication uses clear entry points.
~The page should have only images.#Incorrect. The writing remains important.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker uses a rhetorical question that connects directly to the claim.
Why might this help?{
~It removes the need for evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still necessary.
=It focuses the audience on the central idea.#Correct. A rhetorical question can guide attention when it supports the claim.
~It distracts from the topic.#Incorrect. The question is connected to the claim.
~It replaces organization.#Incorrect. Organization is still needed.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation uses music that covers the speaker's voice.
What is the issue?{
=The multimedia harms audience understanding.#Correct. Multimedia should support, not interfere with, communication.
~The music is automatically credible evidence.#Incorrect. Music is not automatically evidence.
~The speaker should add more music.#Incorrect. More interference would worsen the problem.
~The audience does not need to hear the speaker.#Incorrect. Oral communication requires clear speech.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says the audience is "everyone."
What is the best response?{
~That audience is specific enough for revision.#Incorrect. "Everyone" is too broad to guide choices.
=Name a specific audience so writing and multimedia can be tailored.#Correct. Publication depends on audience-aware choices.
~Audience does not matter online.#Incorrect. Audience still matters.
~Remove all writing to avoid audience issues.#Incorrect. Writing is part of publication.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q14::MLA.ENG3.RS.01
Context: A student uses two sources that support different parts of a claim.
What should the student do?{
~List them without explanation.#Incorrect. Listing is not enough.
~Use the sources as decoration.#Incorrect. Sources support ideas, not decoration.
=Explain how the sources work together to answer the research question.#Correct. This is research synthesis.
~Ignore the research question.#Incorrect. The question guides research.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q15::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: A draft is too broad for a short publication.
Which revision helps most?{
=Focus on one main idea and remove unrelated details.#Correct. This improves clarity and structure.
~Add more unrelated details.#Incorrect. This makes the draft broader.
~Change only the font.#Incorrect. Font does not focus content.
~Remove the claim.#Incorrect. The claim provides focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q16::MLA.ENG3.V.01
Context: A speaker uses the term "credibility."
What should the speaker do if the audience may be unsure of the meaning?{
~Use it without explanation every time.#Incorrect. Repetition does not guarantee understanding.
=Define it briefly and connect it to source quality.#Correct. This makes vocabulary useful for the audience.
~Avoid evidence completely.#Incorrect. Credibility is connected to evidence.
~Use an unrelated definition.#Incorrect. Definitions must fit the context.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q17::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A published page has inconsistent capitalization in headings.
Why revise it?{
~Headings should confuse readers.#Incorrect. Headings should guide readers.
=Consistent capitalization supports readability and polish.#Correct. Conventions matter in publication.
~Capitalization is only needed in handwritten work.#Incorrect. Digital writing also needs conventions.
~The headings should be deleted automatically.#Incorrect. Headings can be useful when corrected.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A presentation conclusion summarizes the claim and main evidence.
Why is this effective?{
=It reinforces the message for the audience.#Correct. A conclusion should help the audience remember the focus.
~It introduces several new claims.#Incorrect. New claims can confuse the ending.
~It avoids the topic.#Incorrect. The conclusion is connected to the topic.
~It removes the need for organization.#Incorrect. The conclusion is part of organization.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q19::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide includes three unrelated images.
What should the student do?{
~Keep all images because more is better.#Incorrect. More media is not always helpful.
=Choose media that supports the claim and remove distractions.#Correct. Multimedia should improve understanding.
~Remove all text and explanation.#Incorrect. Explanation helps the audience.
~Add more unrelated images.#Incorrect. This increases distraction.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q20::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student prepares a final file for online publication.
What does export mean in this context?{
~To erase all source notes#Incorrect. Source notes should remain where needed.
~To remove audience purpose#Incorrect. Audience purpose guides publication.
=To prepare or save the product in a format that can be shared.#Correct. Exporting prepares the product for publication.
~To replace writing with decoration#Incorrect. Exporting does not change the need for quality writing.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker wants the audience to trust the presentation.
Which choice helps most?{
=Use credible evidence and explain why it matters.#Correct. Credible evidence builds audience trust.
~Use unsupported opinions only.#Incorrect. Unsupported opinions weaken credibility.
~Avoid all organization.#Incorrect. Organization supports understanding.
~Use unrelated jokes instead of evidence.#Incorrect. Jokes do not replace support.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student wants to include a map.
When is the map useful?{
~When it has no connection to the claim.#Incorrect. Unrelated maps distract.
~When it is too blurry to read.#Incorrect. Unreadable media cannot help.
=When it clarifies a location or movement connected to the evidence.#Correct. Relevant maps can improve audience understanding.
~When it replaces all writing.#Incorrect. Writing and explanation still matter.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A product is published with no proofreading.
What risk does this create?{
~It guarantees stronger audience trust.#Incorrect. Errors usually reduce trust.
=Errors may distract readers and weaken credibility.#Correct. Proofreading supports publication quality.
~It makes multimedia unnecessary.#Incorrect. Multimedia decisions are separate.
~It improves source accuracy automatically.#Incorrect. Proofreading does not automatically verify sources.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q24::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a caption does not explain the image.
What should the student do?{
~Delete the claim from the writing.#Incorrect. The claim remains important.
~Add an unrelated image.#Incorrect. Unrelated media does not solve the caption problem.
=Revise the caption to connect the image to the claim or evidence.#Correct. This improves clarity and integration.
~Ignore the feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback supports revision.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student describes a strong final Unit 6 product.
Which description is best?{
=It has clear speaking or presentation choices, purposeful multimedia, revised writing, and publication checks for a specific audience.#Correct. This synthesizes Unit 6 communication skills.
~It has decoration but no evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence and explanation matter.
~It has writing that ignores the audience.#Incorrect. Audience awareness is required.
~It has multimedia that is never explained.#Incorrect. Media should support understanding.
}
